  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Isoliquiritigenin may interact with HEp-2 cells through a series of molecular pathways involving UDP-glucuronosyltransferases. Specifically, Isoliquiritigenin is identified as a target of two different UDP-glucuronosyltransferases, 2B15 and 2B10, which in turn are targets of other compounds. These enzymes are known to play a role in drug metabolism and detoxification processes, and their interaction with Isoliquiritigenin may lead to the activation of downstream targets, including HEp-2 cells. The scores from the different paths indicate a consistent but slightly varying level of confidence in this relationship, with the highest score assigned to the interaction involving UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 2B15. The evidence suggests a potential mechanism by which Isoliquiritigenin could influence HEp-2 cells, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct cellular effects."
